# Backend placement (visible vs invisible)

## Test

- **User-driven / visible** (button, form, CRUD they just triggered) → Next.js is fine (Server Actions, thin APIs, feature `server/`).
- **Invisible / external / scheduled** (webhooks, provider callbacks, cron, background jobs) → **Supabase** (Edge Functions, DB triggers, `pg_cron`)—not Next.js/Vercel routes.

## Webhooks and long jobs

Stripe, Postmark, parsers, and similar: webhook **always** lands on Supabase. Verify → update the job/row in the DB. Do **not** put the listener on Vercel so the UI can “update faster.”

**UI sync (project default):** the DB row is the source of truth. While pending, **short-poll / refetch** that row (interval, `router.refresh`, or query `refetchInterval`); **stop** on done/failed. Do **not** default to Supabase Realtime.

**Realtime:** opt-in only when polling is clearly worse (many clients, long waits, collaborative UIs)—never as the reason to move the webhook.

## Cron

Prefer Supabase schedules over Vercel cron for backend-only work.

## Still on Next

RSC page data, auth cookie/`@supabase/ssr` wiring, Server Actions that mutate then revalidate for the current screen. Next calls Supabase; it does not reimplement webhooks or cron.
